WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Before assigning GPU resources, teams should classify the workload.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.800
Not every GPU job has the same purpose.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:15.200
Development notebooks, quick experiments, scheduled training, batch evaluation, data processing, production inference, and urgent operational work each deserve different cost controls.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.400
Experiments should usually be easy to start, time-box, observe, and clean up.

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.800
The goal is learning.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.600
If an experiment becomes important, it can be promoted into a more durable workflow with better tracking.

7
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.600
Until then, default limits and automatic shutdown reduce the chance that exploration becomes unmanaged spend.

8
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.000
Training jobs need stronger reproducibility.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.400
Teams should know the dataset version, code version, configuration, checkpoint plan, success criteria, and stop condition.

10
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.800
A job that cannot be reproduced or evaluated may consume budget without producing a reliable decision.

11
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.600
Inference workloads have a different cost shape.

12
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:59.600
Spend may follow traffic, latency goals, model size, batch behavior, scaling policy, and uptime expectations.

13
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:05.200
The cost target should be connected to service value, not only to hardware utilization.

14
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.000
Production resources should have clear owners, tags, approval paths, and review expectations.

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.200
Classification lets teams tie controls to business intent.

16
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:21.600
The result is a more useful policy than treating every GPU allocation as if it had the same risk and value.

17
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.000
Classification also helps during review.

18
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:36.400
Instead of asking whether a job is expensive in the abstract, teams can ask whether the spend matches the workload class, the expected learning, the service requirement, or the business outcome.
